WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.000
It's called the blockchain.

00:02.000 --> 00:04.000
Blockchain.

00:04.000 --> 00:08.000
Now it's not the most sonorous word in the world,

00:08.000 --> 00:13.000
but I believe that this is now the next generation of the internet,

00:13.000 --> 00:18.000
and that it holds vast promise for every business, every society,

00:18.000 --> 00:20.000
and for all of you individually.

00:20.000 --> 00:25.000
You know, for the past few decades we've had the internet of information,

00:25.000 --> 00:29.000
and when I send you an email or a PowerPoint file or something,

00:29.000 --> 00:31.000
I'm actually not sending you the original.

00:31.000 --> 00:34.000
I'm sending you a copy, and that's great.

00:34.000 --> 00:38.000
This is democratized information.

00:38.000 --> 00:43.000
But when it comes to assets, things like money,

00:43.000 --> 00:47.000
financial assets like stocks and bonds, loyalty points,

00:47.000 --> 00:55.000
intellectual property, music, art, a vote, carbon credit, and other assets,

00:55.000 --> 00:58.000
sending you a copy is a really bad idea.

00:58.000 --> 01:03.000
If I send you $100, it's really important that I don't still have money

01:03.000 --> 01:06.000
and that I can't send it to you.

01:06.000 --> 01:11.000
So this has been called the double-spend problem by cryptographers for a long time.

01:11.000 --> 01:18.000
So today we rely entirely on big intermediaries, middlemen like banks,

01:18.000 --> 01:22.000
governments, big social media companies, credit card companies, and so on,

01:22.000 --> 01:25.000
to establish trust in our economy.

01:25.000 --> 01:30.000
And these intermediaries perform all the business and transaction logic

01:30.000 --> 01:34.000
of every kind of commerce, from authentication, identification of people

01:34.000 --> 01:39.000
through to clearing, settling, and record-keeping.

01:39.000 --> 01:43.000
And overall they do a pretty good job, but there are growing problems.

01:43.000 --> 01:45.000
To begin, they're centralized.

01:45.000 --> 01:48.000
That means that they can be hacked and increasingly are.

01:48.000 --> 01:52.000
And J.P. Morgan, U.S. Federal Government, LinkedIn, Home Depot,

01:52.000 --> 01:54.000
and others found that out the hard way.

01:54.000 --> 01:58.000
They exclude billions of people from the global economy.

01:58.000 --> 02:02.000
For example, people who don't have enough money to have a bank account.

02:02.000 --> 02:04.000
They slow things down.

02:04.000 --> 02:08.000
It can take a second for an email to go around the world,

02:08.000 --> 02:12.000
but it can take days or weeks for money to move through the banking system

02:12.000 --> 02:17.000
across the city, and they take a big piece of the action, 10% to 20%,

02:17.000 --> 02:19.000
just to send money to another country.

02:19.000 --> 02:23.000
They capture our data, and that means that we can't monetize it or use it

02:23.000 --> 02:28.000
to better manage our lives, and our privacy is being undermined.

02:28.000 --> 02:34.000
And the biggest problem is that overall they've appropriated the largess

02:34.000 --> 02:37.000
of the digital age asymmetrically.

02:37.000 --> 02:42.000
And we have wealth creation, but we have growing social inequality.

